<p>A restaurant can't reopen when the building looks clean. It reopens when the kitchen is sanitary, the fire suppression system is recharged and certified, the food has been replaced, and the local health authority signs off. Insurer estimates often price the visible damage and miss the steps in between. We build the claim around what it actually takes to serve food again.</p>

<h2>Kitchen fires and hood systems</h2>
<p>Most restaurant fires start on the cook line. Even a small grease fire that the suppression system puts out triggers a chain of costs:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Recharging and recertifying the hood fire suppression system by a licensed service company</li>
<li>Cleaning or replacing the hood, filters, and exhaust ductwork, all the way to the rooftop fan</li>
<li>Cleaning up suppression agent, which is corrosive, from cooking equipment and surfaces</li>
<li>Testing and repairing fryers, ranges, and other equipment exposed to heat or agent</li>
<li>Repairing the roof penetration and fan if fire reached the duct</li>
</ul>
<p>Grease ducts that carried fire may need inspection before they can be used again, and that inspection should happen before the scope is agreed. Insurers will sometimes argue that grease buildup in the duct shows poor maintenance. Keep your hood cleaning records; they are often the first thing requested. For the fire claim process in general, see <a href="/services/fire-smoke-damage-claims/">fire and smoke damage claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Smoke and grease residue</h2>
<p>Restaurant smoke is different from house-fire smoke. Burning grease and food leave an oily protein residue that sticks to ceilings, walls, ductwork, and the inside of equipment, and it carries a strong odor. Wiping surfaces doesn't remove it. Proper cleanup often involves:</p>
<ul>
<li>Removing and replacing ceiling tiles, insulation, and porous materials in the kitchen and dining room</li>
<li>Cleaning HVAC ducts and replacing filters throughout the building</li>
<li>Deodorization treatments, followed by sealing surfaces before repainting</li>
<li>Cleaning or replacing upholstered seating, drapery, and menus</li>
</ul>
<p>Walk-in coolers and freezers deserve their own line items. Smoke can get into the insulated panels and door gaskets, and a cooler that ran warm during the fire may need its refrigeration system checked by a technician before it holds food again.</p>
<p>Where the insurer's estimate calls for "clean and paint," ask how it handles the residue in places nobody can see, such as above the ceiling and inside the walk-in cooler panels.</p>

