<h2>Food spoilage and contamination</h2>
<p>Restaurants lose food in three ways: a fire or smoke event contaminates stock; a power outage, such as the widespread outages during Hurricane Beryl and Winter Storm Uri, warms the coolers; or a refrigeration unit breaks down. Coverage for each is different. Spoilage from a power outage or mechanical breakdown is often excluded from the base form and covered only by a spoilage endorsement, sometimes with conditions about where the outage began. Food contaminated by a covered fire is more often covered as business personal property.</p>
<p>Before you discard food, photograph the coolers and dry storage, record temperatures if you can, and keep your most recent invoices. A health inspector's order to discard food is strong supporting evidence.</p>

<h2>Health department reopening</h2>
<p>After a fire, sewage backup, or extended outage, many local health departments in Texas require an inspection before a food establishment reopens. Requirements vary by jurisdiction, so call your local health department early and ask what it will need. Common items include sanitizing food-contact surfaces, replacing discarded food, confirming refrigeration holds temperature, and documentation that the fire suppression system has been serviced. Each of those steps takes time, and the time belongs in the business income claim.</p>

<h2>Business income for restaurants</h2>
<p>Restaurant income claims are built from POS data, so they can be precise. We work with your daily sales reports, profit and loss statements, and payroll records to show:</p>
<ul>
<li>Sales lost while closed, based on the same days in prior periods</li>
<li>Payroll you kept paying to hold on to staff, if the policy covers ordinary payroll</li>
<li>Extra costs, like temporary refrigeration, rented equipment, or overtime for a deep clean</li>
<li>Reduced sales after reopening, if the policy includes an extended period of indemnity</li>
</ul>
<p>If you lease, your landlord may be responsible for the roof and structure, while you insure the kitchen equipment, improvements, and income. See the <a href="/commercial-claims/">commercial claims overview</a> for how those policies interact, and our post on <a href="/post/fire-damage-insurance/">fire damage insurance</a> for the basics of a fire claim.</p>
